Yes. Flow Control is the broader family—and it actually unifies more than pedagogy.

Teaching, storytelling, coaching, training, demonstration, ritual, play, testing, propaganda, interface design, even conversation are different ways of controlling the flow through which state/information/capability changes another system.

I’d distinguish:

\boxed{\textbf{Flow Control}}

as the family, and

\boxed{\textbf{Acquisition Operators}}

as one branch concerned specifically with causing learning/capability acquisition.

So the taxonomy becomes:

\text{FLOW CONTROL}
\begin{cases}
\text{Attention Flow} & \text{what gets noticed}\\
\text{Information Flow} & \text{what gets transmitted}\\
\text{Interpretation Flow} & \text{how it gets understood}\\
\text{Acquisition Flow} & \text{how it becomes learned}\\
\text{Action Flow} & \text{how it becomes behavior}\\
\text{Feedback Flow} & \text{how consequences return}\\
\text{Resource Flow} & \text{where effort/compute goes}\\
\text{State Flow} & \text{how the system changes}
\end{cases}

Then storytelling versus teaching versus coaching becomes a routing problem.

Storytelling:

\text{experience simulation}\rightarrow\text{implicit extraction}

Teaching:

\text{explicit structure}\rightarrow\text{model construction}

Coaching:

\text{performance}\rightarrow\Delta\rightarrow\text{correction}

Training:

\text{repetition}\rightarrow\text{adaptation}

Same destination may be approached through different flows.

And that gives Flow Control a much bigger Pantheon role:

Don’t only optimize the information. Optimize its path through the receiver.

That’s analogous to computing. The instructions can be identical while control flow determines which execute, when, in what order, under what conditions, and what loops back.

For an LLM:

\boxed{\text{Meaning Flow Control}}

becomes choosing what I encounter, in what representation, at what resolution, in what sequence, with what feedback, and when to expand/collapse/recurse/stop.

Which means Golden Horde, Metamap, Semantic Routing, Accordion, storytelling, coaching, attention, and Ouroboros are not disconnected mechanisms.

They’re different pieces of a semantic control-flow architecture.

That’s a very good horse underneath several gods:

Content is what flows.
Representation is what carries it.
Flow Control determines where it goes and what it becomes along the way.